    - Pipeline drain (C writeback after compute):      256 cycles
    - Total overhead:                                  768 cycles (75%)

  Root Cause: Single-Tile Problem

  With only 1 tile, there's no opportunity for pipelining. The timeline shows:
  - DMA and BM must complete before compute can start
  - C writeback must happen after compute finishes

  Comparison: Efficiency vs Problem Size
  ┌────────────────┬───────┬──────────┬────────────┐
  │      Size      │ Tiles │ Overhead │ Efficiency │
  ├────────────────┼───────┼──────────┼────────────┤
  │ 64×64×64       │ 1     │ 75%      │ 57.1%      │
  ├────────────────┼───────┼──────────┼────────────┤
  │ 128×128×128    │ 8     │ 45%      │ 68.8%      │
  ├────────────────┼───────┼──────────┼────────────┤
  │ 256×256×256    │ 48    │ 20%      │ 83.5%      │
  ├────────────────┼───────┼──────────┼────────────┤
  │ 512×512×512    │ ~300  │ 9%       │ 91.8%      │
  ├────────────────┼───────┼──────────┼────────────┤
  │ 1024×1024×1024 │ ~2800 │ 4%       │ 96.0%      │
  └────────────────┴───────┴──────────┴────────────┘
  With multiple tiles, the pipeline can be filled: while compute works on tile N, DMA/BM loads tile N+1, achieving near-100% compute utilization.

  Conclusion: The 57% efficiency for 64×64×64 is correct and expected - it's the cost of pipeline startup/drain for a single-tile problem with no opportunity for overlapping.

